\title{coursepack: build tools for plain-text course authoring}
\description{
\code{coursepack} holds the build toolchain that turns a course's declarative
manifests into the artefacts a learning management system can import. It
holds no course content of its own.
}
\section{The contract every entry point keeps}{

\itemize{
\item The first argument is \code{proj}, the course project root. There are no
absolute paths in package code and no default pointing at any one course.
\item Nothing is read from the environment that has a default. Defaults are
function arguments; a \code{Makefile} supplies them.
\item Course-specific facts (module counts, expected item counts, output file
names, known divergences from a reference export) live in the course's own
YAML, never as literals in R.
}

Those three rules are what let one installed copy serve several courses
while keeping them separate. A function that reaches outside its \code{proj}
breaks the separation, so it is a defect regardless of what it was trying to
accomplish.
}

